### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is a **cherry-picking** distortion. While it is technically true for states using a strict "received by Election Day" deadline—such as [Wisconsin](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/supreme-court/supreme-court-won-t-let-late-mail-ballots-count-wisconsin-n1244865) and Michigan—several other states, including [Pennsylvania](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2020/10/19/supreme-court-allows-penn-count-ballots-received-up-nov-6/5899677002/), [California](https://www.democracydocket.com/analysis/mailing-in-your-vote-important-dates/), and Maryland, used "postmarked by" deadlines that explicitly allowed ballots postmarked on or before Election Day to be counted even if they arrived days or weeks afterward (e.g., Pennsylvania accepted ballots up to 3 days after, California up to 17 days). The U.S. Supreme Court even issued a 4-4 ruling in October 2020 that left Pennsylvania's extended receipt deadline intact. By omitting this critical state-by-state variation, the claim falsely implies that *all* such ballots nationwide would be discarded, which was demonstrably not the case.